# {{ app_name }} - Next.js (TypeScript)

## Stack
- Next.js {{ versions.next }} (App Router), React {{ versions.react }}, TypeScript {{ versions.typescript }}, Node {{ versions.node }}
- Tests: Vitest {{ versions.vitest }} with Testing Library
- The API client is generated from `shared/api-contracts/openapi.yml` with `openapi-typescript` and `openapi-fetch`
- Hosting is the project owner's choice (see the `deployment` skill, `.agents/skills/deployment/SKILL.md`)

## Structure
```
{{ app_path }}/app/sign-in/page.tsx      -> "Local development sign-in"
{{ app_path }}/app/api/session/route.ts  -> POST signs in (httpOnly cookie), DELETE signs out
{{ app_path }}/app/page.tsx              -> shows GET /api/me
{{ app_path }}/lib/api/client.ts         -> typed client of the contract
{{ app_path }}/lib/auth/session.ts       -> session cookie helpers
{{ app_path }}/tests/                    -> Vitest unit and component tests
```

## Patterns
- Server Components by default, `"use client"` only for the forms and buttons that need it
- The browser never calls the backend: the session route and the pages call it from the server with the generated client
- The sign-in is the backend's local development identity. It is not complete authentication; replace it with your identity provider (see the `security-auth` skill)
- `audience` is display text only: no route or check reads it
- Keep secrets out of the repository; `API_BASE_URL` is the only runtime variable

## Commands
- `npm run dev` or `task {{ app_id }}:dev` (port {{ port }})
- `npm test` or `task {{ app_id }}:test`
- `npm run lint`, `npm run typecheck`, `npm run build`
- `npm run generate:api` regenerates the client types after a contract change
